Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the analyze gene list feature can be  used to investigate a list of protein  or small molecule identifiers with or  without expression data  the tool performs over-representation in  pathway topology analysis on the input  data  the submission process recognizes many  types of identifiers  as part of the pre-analysis they are  mapped to react home molecules  the ideal identifiers to use are uniprot  ids for proteins  chebi ids for small molecules and either  hgnc gene symbols or ensemble ids for  dna  or rna molecules as these are our main  external reference sources for proteins  in small molecules  there are two types of data format that  can be submitted  to submit the qualitative data the data  should be a single column of identifiers  such as uniprot ids  gene symbols or chebi ids  they are matched to react home pathways  and tested for over-representation  and pathway topology to submit the  quantitative data  the data should have a column of  identifiers as before and one or more  additional columns of numbers such as  expression or dose response values  this will be recognized as quantitative  data and an additional overlay process  will be run along with over  representation  selecting the continue button on the  analysis submission form brings you to  an options page  several popular proteomics tools  generate results that contain a mixture  of human and other species identifiers  when the default option of project to  human is selected  all non-human identifiers in your query  are converted to their human orthologous  equivalents  as reactome pathways represent human  events  this conversion will maximize the  chances of a successful match to a  reactor  pathway however you may want to uncheck  this box  if your query represents data from an  infected tissue such as a mixture of  human and microbial identifiers  to prevent conversion of non-human  identifiers to human  uncheck the box the include interactor's  option is unchecked by default  with this option selected reacton  pathways are expanded by including all  protein protein  interactors from the intact database for  every protein represented by the pathway  this can greatly increase the size of  the pathway and extend it to include  proteins that would not otherwise be  represented  which maximizes the chances of matching  your submitted identifiers to the  resulting expanded pathways  however the risk of this pathway  expansion is that the expanded protein  set has not been verified by manual  curation and review  you should understand that intact  interactions may be false positives due  to artifacts resulting from the  technology  used with no biological relevance  for single column input list of protein  or small molecule identifiers  over representation and pathway topology  analyses are performed  the names of the columns are explained  when you hover over the column name on  the react home web page  analysis results are shown in the  analysis tab within the details panel  all reacton pathways are shown in blocks  of 20 pathways  ordered by ascending false discovery  rate or fdr value obtained from  over-representation analysis  in addition the number of molecules  matched to total number of molecules and  fdr values are added to the right side  of pathway names in the pathway  hierarchy panel  reactions that contain any of the  matching molecules are boxed in orange  when you click on the name of a pathway  in the analysis tab  the pathway hierarchy will expand to  show it and its name will be highlighted  in dark blue  if it has sub pathways you can use the  plus buttons to the left of the pathway  name to reveal sub pathways and  reactions  analysis results can be filtered for  various factors  including pathway size significance  threshold  p-value and species using the  funnel-shaped filter icon on the top  right corner of the details panel  by default all molecules are used for  over-representation analysis  subtypes of molecule can be selected  from a drop-down list in the filter  option  selecting these subsets will redisplay  results that consider  only the selected molecular subtype  another option in the filter allows  users to ignore disease pathways for the  analysis  as it might skew the results in some  cases  the analysis tab in the details panel  has three buttons on its left  by default the results button is  selected  below this is the not found button that  indicates the number of submitted  identifiers that were not successfully  matched to molecules in react home  click on the button to produce a list if  a significant portion of the identifiers  could not be matched to pathways then  either the identifiers are not  compatible to use with reactome and you  should replace them with something more  suitable  or the identifiers represent molecules  that are not found in our pathways  but might be connected to a known or  unknown definable process  analysis results are also reflected in  the diagrams of pathways and reactions  in the diagram panel above the details  panel  under the network view each pathway in  the network view of pathways is colored  in different shades of yellow according  to the over-representation significance  or p-value  a selection bar at the bottom of the  diagram panel will allow you to color  pathways  based on the coverage which reflect the  number of matched entities  instead of p-value under the voronoi  view  the voronoi map view show the same  information as in the network view  but this view is easier for users to  visualize the results  textbook style diagrams represent  analysis results within the label for  sub pathways  the label background changes from blue  to white a yellow band is used to  indicate the proportion of the pathway  that is represented in the query data  set  a gray bar above the label indicates the  number of pathway entities that is  represented in the query data set  the total number of entities in the  pathway and the fdr corrected  probability score  under the sbgn diagram entities in the  diagram  are colored yellow if they were  represented in the submitted data set  complexes sets and sub pathway icons are  colored to represent the proportion of  the molecules they contain that are  represented in the submitted identifier  list  to examine the details of a complex or  set hover the mouse pointer over it  a small blue triangle will appear on the  right side  select this to open an information panel  the uppermost tab on the left size will  be selected the molecules represented by  the complex or set or displayed as a  table  with a yellow shaded background if they  were in the query data set  if you submit data in a format that  includes columns of numeric values  following a first column of protein  compound or other suitable identifiers  such as probe ids  the analysis tool will interpret your  data as expression data  the numeric values are used to color  objects in pathway diagrams  the results page is very similar to that  of a qualitative input analysis with  single column list of identifiers with  additional columns in the analysis  details  these extra columns represent the  submitted expression values  clicking on a pathway name launches the  relevant pathway diagram  things to look for include objects in  the pathway diagram are colored  according to the numeric values  submitted  the gradient yellow to blue colors are  based on a scale  which is represented in a bar on the  right hand side  when zoomed out objects are colored to  reflect the average of the values  submitted for the molecules  when zoomed in complexes and sets will  resolve to colored bands  representing the values of individual  molecules  the experiment browser toolbar is used  to step through the columns of your data  such time points or disease progression  as you move across the different columns  the pathway diagrams will recolor to  reflect the new values

